I. Introduction
A. Background
In recent years, consumers of small multirotor aircraft have progressed from hobbyists and recreational users to commercial businesses, militaries, and law enforcement agencies. On a larger scale, companies have invested significant resources to develop electric vertical takeoff and landing (eVTOL) rotorcraft for urban air mobility (UAM) and cargo delivery applications. These developments have increased the complexity of proposed operations of UAS and eVTOL systems, which include beyond visual line of site (BVLOS) and low-altitude flight plans over densely populated areas. Demonstration of safe and efficient operations will be critical to realize their full potential and increase public acceptance. In support of advancing flight safety assurance capabilities, the NASA System Wide Safety project is investigating the concept of In-Time Aviation Safety Management Systems (IASMS) [1]. This concept extends traditional SMS to provide more timely and predictive risk assessments and mitigation. To help evaluate how IASMS may be applied to sUAS and UAM domains, a Flight Performance Assessment Service (FPAS) has been developed that can aid pre-flight and in-flight trajectory planning. Primary outputs of FPAS include energy reserve predictions, simulated vehicle trajectories, and identification of hazardous flight conditions for a user-defined flight plan. The utility of FPAS is dependent on the accuracy of multirotor flight dynamics and performance models. Areas of aerodynamic modeling that have been identified as technical gaps in the existing FPAS simulation framework are i) the representation of propeller-propeller aerodynamic interactions and ii) the understanding of the effects of angular rate on dynamic stability. It is especially important to understand how these effects impact trajectory prediction, power consumption modeling, and control system design. Increased knowledge of these subjects is also expected to aid future designs in terms of safety, efficiency, range, and payload capacity.
B. Relevant Prior Work
A number of recent studies have used wind tunnel experiments, mid-fidelity flow solvers, and high-fidelity computational fluid dynamics (CFD) to better understand complex interactional aerodynamics. Stokkermans et al. [2] investigated an array of two-propeller systems that are representative of eVTOL designs. One such system consisted of a side-by-side propeller unit that could be tested at different hub separation distances, advance ratios, and propeller angles of attack. Measurements from wind tunnel experiments and comparisons to an extended blade element momentum (BEM) model showed rear propeller thrust and power reductions up to 30% relative to the isolated propeller performance. Wind tunnel results from Zanotti et al. [3] corroborated performance degradations of the rear propeller in a tandem configuration and reported that increasing the vertical hub separation distance alleviates adverse interaction effects in transition phases. Several other works have reported similar findings regarding vertical hub spacing for propellers in proximity [4,5,6]. Side-by-side rotors have also been studied by Shukla and Komerath [7] in hover conditions using particle image velocimetry (PIV) and performance measurements. The time-averaged PIV data indicate that the tip vortices interact between adjacent rotors in close proximity, resulting in a loss of their coherent structure and a deviation in trajectory relative to that of an isolated rotor wake. The effects of inter-rotor wake interactions were more significant at lower Reynolds numbers, which was attributed to weaker vortices in terms of circulation and downwash. A multirotor testbed (MTB) was presented by Russel et al. [8] which has capability to vary hub positions and cant angles of up to six individual rotors. There have been multiple test campaigns to demonstrate the MTB capability including comparisons to simulation tools [9,10]. The experimental results from [2-10] have provided insight to the order of magnitude of interaction effects and laid a foundation for future research in this area. Generally, wind-tunnel studies have primarily involved small scale propellers or rotors, resulting in large Reynolds number differences in comparison to larger eVTOL configurations. This is in part due to practical challenges and significant resources required to test full scale rotors. High-fidelity CFD has served as a useful tool to study interaction effects with similitude parameters representative of larger eVTOL vehicles. Healy et al. studied in-line two-rotor units using a commercial Navier Stokes solver with a detached eddy simulation model [11,12]. The simulation results showed that the front rotor wake induces downwash on the aft rotor, leading to a lift deficit and a significant change in hub pitching moment. Longitudinally canting the rotors outward positions the front of the aft rotor disk farther away from the front rotor wake, alleviating thrust
