reduction. NASA researchers have also used high-fidelity CFD to simulate small scale multicopters [13,14] and full scale eVTOL vehicles in hover and transition phases [15,16]. The use of Reynolds-averaged Navier Stokes (RANS) and large-eddy simulations has enabled high-fidelity analysis of these complex flows. However, these simulations are often computationally expensive, which poses challenges regarding their application to conceptual design or rapid stability and control assessments. As an alternative to mesh-based CFD approaches, the viscous vortex particle method (VVPM) has been used within the rotorcraft community to capture important characteristics of the wake structure. For example, Alvarez and Ning [17] demonstrated the application of VVPM to capture multirotor aerodynamic interactions with applications to eVTOL design. The existing literature describing the effects of angular rate on multirotor stability and control characteristics using experimental data is limited. Baris et al. reported static and dynamic wind tunnel results of a small UAS (sUAS) quadcopter [18], including PIV and performance measurements [19, 20]. Static wind tunnel measurements showed nonlinear body-axis pitching moment trends at positive angles of attack (nose up relative to the flow) and were attributed to changes in aft rotor performance. Dynamic testing was completed for a range of oscillation frequencies and body-axis pitching moment hysteresis loops were presented with nonlinear characteristics and advance ratio dependencies [20]. A nonlinear body-axis pitching moment trend was also observed in static wind tunnel testing of an octocopter [21] and several quadcopter configurations [22,23]. This trend warrants further investigation of the individual propulsor performance to understand how interaction effects impact the total vehicle body-axis moments and stability characteristics. While the preceding studies have investigated propulsive aerodynamic interaction effects in experimental and simulated campaigns, there is still a gap in data resolution and a need to expand on conditions previously reported in the literature in terms of advance ratio, relative flow angle, and hub separation distance. In addition, a database of propulsor performance in dynamic conditions would be useful to improve the understanding of multirotor dynamic stability characteristics for control system design and serve as a dataset to advance aerodynamic prediction tools. This research seeks to enhance the database for understanding aerodynamic interactions to improve high-fidelity modeling capabilities.
C. Objectives and Approach
This paper presents an aerodynamic interaction study of a tandem propeller configuration using wind tunnel experiments. The primary objectives were to quantify the effect of aerodynamic interactions on propeller thrust and identify conditions where effects are most significant. A secondary objective was to obtain a preliminary dataset from dynamic test conditions to assess the impact of angular rate on thrust performance. Experiments utilizing static and forced oscillation techniques were conducted in the NASA Langley 12-Foot Low-Speed Tunnel. Individual propeller forces, moments, and electric power consumption were measured at different hub separation distances, advance ratios, and relative flow angles. A high resolution of force and moment data was targeted in conditions where the front propeller wake transitions below, into, and above the rear propeller disk to capture nonlinear trends. Flow visualization with smoke and high-speed cameras were used to qualitatively assess the wake skew angle of the front propeller and inflow properties entering the rear propeller disk. Dynamic testing was focused on angle of attack regions with significant aerodynamic interactions and included variations of advance ratio, oscillation frequency, and amplitude. This paper is organized as follows. Section II outlines the experimental setup including a description of the test rig, model installation, instrumentation, and test conditions. Section III presents static and dynamic test results including performance measurements and flow visualization. Section IV summarizes the key findings and outlines areas of future research.
II. Experimental Setup A. Test Rig Description
A test rig was designed to mount two propeller assemblies at arbitrary separation distances along a horizontal rail. Figure 1 shows an exploded view of a single propeller assembly. At the base, a rail adapter attaches to a six-inch vertical post to increase the height of the propeller disk plane and minimize interference between the propeller wake and the horizontal rail. A two-inch spacer block was machined to provide additional vertical clearance and connect the vertical post to the non-metric end of a six-axis strain gauge force balance. A fairing was designed to house the balance and fastens with two bolts to the front of the non-metric end. The fairing was 3D-printed using polylactic acid (PLA) material and has an oval cross-section that tapers at both ends. A routing hole was placed on the trailing side to pass motor power cables through and avoid exposure to the freestream air. The fairing was required to prevent abrupt flow separation and vortices that would result from flow passing over the sharp edges of the balance. In addition, it prevents flow from passing over the strain gauges which could cause inaccurate force and moment measurements. The motor and propeller are mounted at the top of the assembly to the metric end of the balance. The
