B. Model Installation and Instrumentation
The experiments were performed in the NASA Langley 12-Foot Low-Speed Tunnel. The test section is 15 feet in length and 12 feet in height and width with an octagonal cross section. This size is suitable to study many sUAS configurations and subscale eVTOL vehicles. The facility supports free-flight, static, and dynamic test techniques with a maximum operating freestream dynamic pressure (̅q) of approximately ̅q = 7 lbf/ft2. Figure 4 shows front and side views of the test rig mounted to a sting at α = 0° and d = 3.1R. The sting attaches to a forced oscillation system (FOS) and a C-strut. The FOS is driven by an electric motor equipped with encoders for position measurements. The FOS can be used in a static pitch-pause mode or in a dynamic mode with sinusoidal oscillations at a specified frequency and amplitude. In these experiments, the FOS was used to orient the test rig for static and dynamic testing. The front propeller rotates clockwise (CW), and the rear propeller rotates counterclockwise (CCW), as viewed from the top. The rear propeller assembly was also tested in an isolated configuration to better understand the effects of the horizontal rail and front propeller fairing on rear propeller performance. The isolated propeller assembly is shown mounted directly to the sting in Fig. 5 at α = −40° and α = 0°. Each propeller assembly was equipped with a strain gauge force balance for individual performance measurements. The force and moment measurements were sampled at 2500 Hz. The static and dynamic axial load ranges (direction of thrust) of the strain gauge balance are ±50 and ±25 lbf, respectively. The electric motor cables were routed through the fairing, along the sting, and outside of the test section where they connected to two electronic speed controllers (ESC) and a 30 Volt power supply. Each ESC connected to microcontrollers that enabled communication to the data acquisition system.
