A requirement for these experiments was to maintain desired motor speeds at arbitrary aerodynamic blade loading conditions. To achieve this, a closed-loop control system was developed to regulate motor speeds using measurement feedback from each ESC. Motor rotational frequency (Ω) was estimated onboard the instrumentation from discrete pulses output from the ESCs during each electrical commutation. To develop a control system, a motor dynamics model G(s) was identified from bench test data. Programmed square-wave frequency sweep inputs were injected into each ESC to drive the motor speed to oscillate around 7500 RPM. A first-order transfer function with time delay was fit to this data to model the motor speed response to pulse width modulation (PWM) inputs, and a control system was designed based on this model. Figure 6 presents a block diagram of the control system structure. The input to the block diagram is a user-specified reference motor speed in revolutions per second (nref). The control system contains both forward path and feedback path elements. On the forward path, a second order low-pass filter F(s) shapes and smooths changes in the reference command between test points to avoid high dynamic loads from vibration. A static cubic function KF(n) computes the predicted steady state PWM required to achieve the reference motor speed. This function was identified from time-averaged steady state measurements of the PWM at various motor speeds, shown in Fig. 7. The forward path elements were tuned manually to smoothly track changes in the reference motor speed. On the feedback path, proportional and integral feedback gains (Kp and KI) regulate the error between the filtered reference motor speed and the measured motor speed (n). The feedback gains were designed to achieve gain and phase margins of 9 dB and 90 deg and to maximize the disturbance rejection bandwidth (DRB), which is defined as the -3 dB crossing frequency of the sensitivity function of the closed-loop system, shown in Fig. 8. The sensitivity function describes the motor speed response to output noise. Table 2 provides the controller gains and transfer functions for the plant model and command filter.
