C. Wind Tunnel Test Conditions 1. Static Testing
The static test points were selected based on angle of attack (α), separation distance (d), and propeller advance ratio (J). The propeller advance ratio (J = V/(nD)) is a nondimensional parameter commonly used to characterize propeller aerodynamic performance. Table 3 lists the propeller rotational speed and freestream conditions of the wind tunnel test section for each advance ratio case under standard atmospheric temperature and pressure. It is important to note that conducting the test with multiple rotational speeds to achieve the desired advance ratio settings also causes variations in the propeller blade Reynolds number and tip Mach number. The impact of propeller blade Reynolds number and tip Mach number effects is not examined in this paper and the following analysis assumes the magnitude of aerodynamic interaction effects are primarily due to changes in advance ratio, angle of attack, and separation distance. Angle of attack sweeps were performed at a specified hub separation distance and advance ratio. The test rig was aligned with the freestream velocity (i.e., no lateral offset in hub spacing). The front and rear motors rotated CW and CCW, respectively. Two angle of attack schedules (αsched) were used with each containing 20 steps:
αsched1 = [-60,-45,-30, -20,-10, 0, 10, 15, 20, 25, 27.5, 30, 32.5 ,35, 37.5, 40, 42.5, 45, 47.5, 50] deg
αsched2 = [-60,-50,-40, -30,-20, -15,-10, -5, 0, 5, 10, 15, 17.5, 20, 22.5, 25, 27.5, 30, 35, 40 ] deg
The first angle of attack schedule (αsched1) was used for the lowest advance ratio case of J = 0.23, while the second angle of attack schedule (αsched2) was used for all other advance ratios. The primary difference between the schedules is the region of increased resolution and the maximum angle of attack. The finer-resolution portion of each schedule was designed to center around the point of maximum rear propeller thrust degradation. For each angle of attack, the FOS positioned the test rig at the desired orientation. The test rig settled for three seconds before data was recorded for five seconds. The settling and record time were sufficient to allow the motor speed control system to reach the desired steady state response and avoid significant variations in the moving average of the strain gauge balance signal. The force and moment data was sampled at 2500 Hz, the motor rotational speed was sampled 100 Hz, and the motor current and voltage were sampled at 100 Hz. The selected force and moment sample rate was the highest available rate for the data acquisition system and was selected to allow for power spectral density (PSD) analysis at frequencies greater than the motor rotational speed. The motor speed control system was designed to operate at 100 Hz which determined the sample rate of the power measurements. The hub separation distances that were tested were:
