III. Results
This section presents the thrust coefficient (CT = T/(ρn2D4)) measurements for the isolated propeller and tandem propeller configurations. The power consumption measurements and force and moment data from the other axes are not presented in this paper. Three phases of static testing were completed using a component build-up methodology. In phase one, the isolated propeller thrust coefficient (CTISO) was measured to provide a baseline dataset without the presence of the horizontal rail and a fairing located upstream (see Fig. 5). In phase two, the thrust coefficient of the rear propeller in the tandem configuration was measured with the front motor turned off. This term is referred to as CTRear ISO and contains interference effects from the front fairing and horizontal rail. In phase three, the front and rear motors operated at constant, equal rotational speeds. The measured coefficients from this phase are referred to as CTFront and CTRear. The following sub-sections present results and discussion from each phase of testing.
A. Isolated Propeller
Figure 9 shows the isolated propeller thrust coefficient (CTISO) versus angle of attack for J ε [0.23, 0.67]. The isolated propeller configuration was not tested at J = 0.75. The sensitivity of the thrust coefficient to angle of attack increases with advance ratio. For many multirotor vehicles, lower advance ratios correspond to hover and low-speed conditions, while higher advance ratios may correspond to forward flight or transition regions. The large variation in thrust coefficient in this data highlights the importance of modeling incidence angle and advance ratio effects in propulsion models used in multirotor flight dynamics simulations. These vehicles may encounter omnidirectional relative flow angles spanning hover, transition, forward flight, vertical climbs, and descents. For example, free-flight measurements of a quadcopter showed that the vehicle will trim with negative angles of attack in forward flight [29]. The vehicle may abruptly transition to a positive angle of attack during maneuvering, or trim at a positive angle of attack in a vertical descent during forward flight [30].
