Figure 11 presents a surface plot of the isolated propeller thrust coefficient as a function of the nondimensional velocity components μx and μz. The thrust coefficient varies significantly with μz due to changes in magnitude and direction of axial flow entering the propeller disk. Axial flow directly impacts the local flow angle on the blades and the lift produced. The edgewise flow component also causes a change in the local blade angle of attack and an asymmetric nonuniform induced velocity distribution across the propeller disk [31]. The velocity components impact the characteristics of the wake, which consists of sheets of vorticity that roll up into tip vortices and are carried rearward by the freestream flow [32]. Increases in edgewise flow may cause an increase skew of the wake relative to the disk plane. The wake skew angle (χ = tan−1(μ/λ)), is depicted in Fig. 12 as the angle between the negative zh direction and the direction of the total velocity component at the disk center (vT) [33]. The inflow ratio (λ) is a function of the wake-induced velocity at the rotor disk center and may be approximated using Glauert’s momentum theory with an iterative approach, such as Newton-Raphson [34, 35].
