C. Tandem Configuration
Comparisons of rear propeller thrust measurements between the second and third phases of testing (i.e., CTRear ISO and CTRear) were made to assess the influence of propeller-propeller aerodynamic interactions. The impact of angle of attack, advance ratio, and hub separation distance on rear propeller thrust variations are discussed in the following sub-sections.
1. Angle of attack effects
The extent of propeller-propeller aerodynamic interactions varies significantly with angle of attack. Figure 14(a) shows the thrust coefficient of the rear propeller assembly in the tandem and isolated configuration at an advance ratio of J = 0.47 and a hub separation distance of d = 3.1R. The relative thrust degradation at each condition is depicted in Fig. 14(b). An underlying assumption in this analysis is that propeller-propeller interactions are the primary cause of differences between CTRear and CTRear ISO. In practice, the velocity field produced by the front propeller may impact the test rig interference effect. As such, CTISO is also presented in Fig. 14. The comparison between CTRear and CTISO provides an estimated bound on the interference effects if the test rig interference were negligible due to the effect of the front propeller. The thrust coefficients CTRear and CTRear ISO match within 5% for angles of attack αε[-60°,-15°], suggesting minimal propeller-propeller aerodynamic interaction effects in this range. Thrust degradations of the rear propeller in the tandem configuration becomes significant as the angle of attack increases above α = -15°, producing approximately 30% less thrust relative to the isolated configurations. As α increases from 15 deg to 40 deg, CTRear gets progressively closer to CTRear ISO. The trend of CTRear/ CTRear ISO versus angle of attack is likely due to the relative vertical spacing between the front and rear propeller disk planes as the test rig changes angle of attack. At negative angles of attack (nose down relative to the flow), the rear propeller is above the front propeller, and avoids the majority of the front propeller wake. Whereas at positive angles of attack, the rear propeller is oriented beneath the front propeller and experiences altered inflow properties propagated from the front propeller wake.
