D. Forced Oscillation
This section presents several examples of data from the forced oscillation testing. The tare with the motors turned off was subtracted from the corresponding wind-on dataset with the same oscillation frequency (f) and amplitude. The FOS position and thrust measurements were filtered using a 6th order lowpass Butterworth filter with a cutoff frequency equal to six times the oscillation frequency. The thrust measurements were grouped into bins for each cycle and nondimensionalized to compute CT with the mean motor speed measurements. Figure 19 presents examples of the dynamic data for oscillation frequencies of f = 0.8 Hz and f = 1.2 Hz at an advance ratio of J = 0.47. The front and rear thrust coefficients are plotted for each cycle of oscillation alongside the static measurements for the same freestream conditions. The thickness of the hysteresis loop indicates the magnitude of the damping effects, and the shape designates either linear or nonlinear characteristics [24]. For example, elliptical shaped loops designate linear behavior. The sign of the dynamic derivative is determined by assessing the direction of the hysteresis loop. The dynamic derivative is positive if the hysteresis loop follows a clockwise direction and is negative if it follows a counterclockwise direction [24]. The maximum and minimum angles of attack during the oscillation are the points with the highest angular acceleration, which can cause the ends of the hysteresis loops to deviate from the static data. A comparison of Fig. 19(a) and Fig. 19(b) shows the thickness of the hysteresis loops slightly increase with oscillation frequency at constant advance ratio. However, the dynamic data matches relatively closely with the static data in both cases, suggesting that the dynamic motion did not significantly vary the thrust performance for these conditions. The front propeller thrust coefficient primarily shows linear behavior across the entire angle of attack range, while the rear thrust coefficient contains nonlinear characteristics in the regions of thrust degradation. For example the shape of the front and rear thrust coefficient hysteresis loops vary significantly at α = 10°.
