IV. Summary and Future Work
Results from static and dynamic wind tunnel tests of a tandem propeller configuration were presented. The effects of angle of attack, advance ratio, and hub separation distance were investigated. Preliminary dynamic results were presented using a forced oscillation technique showing variations of the hysteresis loops for the front and rear thrust coefficients at different amplitudes, frequencies, advance ratios, and angle of attack. The results showed that the most significant rear propeller thrust degradations occurred at positive angles of attack near α = 15° (nose up relative to the flow) and advance ratios of J = 0.47 and J = 0.58. The advance ratio impacts the angle of attack region of interaction effects, and qualitative flow visualization results suggest it is correlated with the wake skew angle of the front propeller in relation to the rear propeller disk plane. As the advance ratio increases, the onset of the adverse interaction effects occurs at a lower angle of attack. Increasing the hub separation distance alleviates some of the adverse effects; however, significant performance degradations can still occur. Forced oscillation results showed that the front propeller exhibited reasonably linear behavior across the conditions tested, whereas the rear propeller exhibited dynamic nonlinearity in the region of significant thrust degradation. The static aerodynamic interaction effects are currently being incorporated into a lookup table to assess their impact on trim predictions using the quadcopter simulation from Ref. [22] for forward flight conditions. The assumption in this implementation is that the interaction effects only occur between the pair of front and rear propellers. Any interaction effects between adjacent propellers would be ignored, limiting this approach to forward flight with no crosswinds. In addition, comparisons of the static and dynamic data to existing aerodynamic prediction tools using the viscous vortex particle method are in progress. Other data reduction activities include assessment of the frequency content of the rear propeller thrust coefficient in regions of aerodynamic interaction, analysis of the offaxis hub moments and power consumption data, and parameter estimation of dynamic aerodynamic coefficients using
